          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life</strong><br>\nÉdouard Manet was born on January 23, 1832, in Paris, into an affluent and well-connected family. His father was a judge, and his mother was the goddaughter of the Swedish crown prince. Manet was destined for a career in law but defied his father's wishes to become an artist.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Art Education</strong><br>\nManet studied under the academic painter Thomas Couture and copied the old masters in the Louvre. This classical training, combined with his interest in contemporary subjects, laid the foundation for his unique style.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Break with Tradition</strong><br>\nManet's paintings were controversial and often rejected by the official art exhibitions of the time. His works \"Le déjeuner sur l'herbe\" and \"Olympia\" caused public outrage due to their modern content, unconventional painting style, and bold rejection of classical norms.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
        "slideBackgroundSelector": {}
      },
      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
