            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Health Challenges</strong><br>\nManet's health began to decline in the 1870s, suffering from the effects of syphilis and rheumatism. This led to the amputation of his left leg in 1883, after which his health rapidly deteriorated.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Death and Legacy</strong><br>\nÉdouard Manet died in Paris on April 30, 1883. Despite the mixed reception during his career, today he is celebrated as a pivotal figure in the transition from Realism to Impressionism and is recognized for his profound impact on the course of modern art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Influence and Rememberance</strong><br>\nManet's influence extends beyond Impressionism and is evident in the broad spectrum of later artists who drew inspiration from his work. His bold approach to painting and social themes has cemented his reputation in art history as an innovator and a master of modern art.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
